WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.120
All right, let's get right into audience questions.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.140
Uh, some really good ones this week.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:09.000
First question, isolationism versus non interventionism.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.000
This is a good topic.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.839
Hey David, I hear lots of talk when people
discuss Trump's tariffs about isolationism.

00:00:15.839 --> 00:00:18.750
Is this the same as non interventionism?

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:19.750
It is not.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.830
And this is a really, really important distinction.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:27.920
I am a non interventionist when it comes to
the United States using the military or using

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.570
other means to mettle in other countries,
whether it comes to being the world's policeman

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:38.340
when there is not actually a coalition, for
example, that is decided this is something

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.160
that the world is going to take on and it's
abundantly clear that that's what should be

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:42.160
done.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.710
I am against the United States meddling in
the elections of other countries or in regime

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:49.530
change in other countries.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:54.070
But I am not an isolation is because I see
isolationism as a much further step, which

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:59.070
is not only do we not get involved militarily
for the defense of a third country, uh, not

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:04.361
only do we not do all of the things that a
non interventionist would avoid doing, but

00:01:04.361 --> 00:01:08.110
isolationists can also want to be isolationist
on trade.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:10.320
Now, I believe in specialization.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.810
I believe that given the drastically Annaik
unequal distribution of resources throughout

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:22.430
the world, which includes both, uh, resources
of all kinds, I mean plant and animal resources.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:28.830
If you read Jared diamond's book, guns, germs
and steel, he outlines how it is not by chance

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:33.100
that some parts of the world became more successful
and earlier than others.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:38.690
It is significantly related to the flora and
fauna that either did or didn't allow the

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:44.250
creation of agriculture, which developed food
surpluses, which allowed specialist to start

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:49.540
developing technology and culture and politics
and governmental type organizations, et Cetera,

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:50.600
et cetera.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.030
So I'm not an isolationist when it comes to
trade.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:59.640
Uh, I also, I'm not an isolationist when it
comes to dealing with refugee issues around

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.570
the world dealing with climate change.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:07.450
If you are a pure and true isolationists,
you feel no responsibility to even consider

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:11.310
doing anything about refugee crises in other
places in the world.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.850
As a non interventionist.

00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:18.010
You might choose not to get involved militarily
in what may be taking place in some other

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:25.130
countries, but when, what those circumstances
do is generate refugees as a non isolationist

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:31.530
but non interventionists you can say here's
my how my country is going to get involved

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.270
in helping refugees for example.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:40.340
Isolationism can also lead to very extreme
immigration policies for example, which I

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:41.340
don't favor.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:47.849
So there are some really important differences
between being an isolationist and a non interventionist.

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:55.390
I consider myself a non interventionist who
sees military action as the absolute last

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:59.750
resort, uh, to be used only in very particular
circumstances.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:01.620
But I am the last thing from an isolationist.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.360
And then lastly, I mentioned climate change.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:13.440
Right now the pollution, climate change disaster
that we are dealing with goes completely beyond

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:14.930
country borders.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:21.350
And isolationism is also a way to, in a sense,
bury our heads in the sand if it wants to

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:27.730
be used this way and say, you know, we're
worried about our country, we don't care about

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:31.459
pollution going elsewhere even though we have
to deal with pollution coming here from other

00:03:31.459 --> 00:03:34.730
countries because pollution doesn't respect
country lines.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:38.060
So I think isolationism is an absolute disaster.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:44.849
I think non interventionism is very much in
line with the social democracy, a philosophy

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:47.580
that I sort of by and large subscribe to.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.560
Let me know if I'm missing anything or if
I'm getting anything wrong or if you simply

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:51.129
disagree with my conclusions.

